      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Research in sociology and psychology demonstrates that standardized tests often reflect pre-existing socioeconomic advantages more than innate ability, as they correlate strongly with family income and parental education. The very concept of \"merit\" in social reproduction is contested because these metrics fail to account for systemic barriers and the unequal distribution of cultural capital, effectively legitimizing privilege under the guise of fairness. Furthermore, the over-reliance on IQ and testing in policy is criticized for pathologizing minority success and ignoring the complex, non-linear nature of human potential and achievement.",
